{"causes": ["The intellectual ferment in Athens during the 5th century BCE, particularly the rise of sophistry and philosophy, contributed to Phaedrus's engagement with Socrates.", "The political and religious tensions of the Peloponnesian War created a climate where accusations of impiety, such as the profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries, were used for political ends.", "Plato's decision to feature Phaedrus prominently in his dialogues ensured his enduring historical and philosophical recognition, independent of his direct actions."], "effects": ["Phaedrus's exile from Athens contributed to the broader political instability and purges affecting the Socratic circle during the Peloponnesian War.", "His portrayal in Plato's *Symposium* and *Phaedrus* dialogue established him as a canonical character in Western philosophy, central to discussions on love, rhetoric, and the soul.", "The historical accounts of his involvement in the Eleusinian Mysteries scandal provide crucial insight into the religious and legal systems of classical Athens."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "socrates", "sourceName": "Socrates", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "phaedrus", "targetName": "Phaedrus", "context": "Socrates profoundly influenced Phaedrus's philosophical development and intellectual pursuits in Athens during the late 5th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "phaedrus", "sourceName": "Phaedrus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "profanation-of-eleusinian-mysteries", "targetName": "Profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries", "context": "Phaedrus was indicted for his alleged participation in the profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries in 415 BCE, leading to his exile."}, {"sourceSlug": "plato", "sourceName": "Plato", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "phaedrus-dialogue", "targetName": "Phaedrus (Plato's Dialogue)", "context": "Plato immortalized Phaedrus as a central character in his eponymous dialogue, which explores themes of love, rhetoric, and the soul."}, {"sourceSlug": "peloponnesian-war", "sourceName": "Peloponnesian War", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "phaedrus", "targetName": "Phaedrus", "context": "Phaedrus's significant life events, including his indictment and exile, occurred during the tumultuous Peloponnesian War (431–404 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "phaedrus", "sourceName": "Phaedrus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "athens-greece", "targetName": "Athens, Greece", "context": "Phaedrus lived and participated in the intellectual and political life of Athens, Greece, until his exile around 415 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Primary residence and intellectual center"}, {"name": "Eleusis, Greece", "role": "Site of alleged sacrilege"}, {"name": "Thessaly, Greece", "role": "Region of exile"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Phaedrus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "phaedrus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q554535", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaedrus_(Athenian)"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Phaedrus holds significant historical importance as a key figure in the Socratic circle and, crucially, as a character whose philosophical discussions in Plato's dialogues fundamentally shaped Western thought on love, rhetoric, and the soul, influencing countless subsequent philosophers and literary figures.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:27:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Phaedrus, son of Pythocles, of the Myrrhinus deme, was an ancient Athenian aristocrat associated with the inner-circle of the philosopher Socrates. He was indicted in the profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries in 415 BC during the Peloponnesian War, causing him to flee Athens. Phaedrus was a philosophers & thinkers from Athens (443 BCE, Athens–392 BCE). Recognized as a landmark figure with global significance (referenced in 0 Wikipedia editions). Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "Phaedrus, son of Pythocles, of the Myrrhinus deme (c. 443 BCE – 392 BCE), was a prominent Athenian aristocrat and a key member of the intellectual circle surrounding the philosopher Socrates. His historical significance stems not only from his direct involvement in the turbulent politics and religious controversies of late 5th-century BCE Athens but, perhaps more enduringly, from his vivid portrayal as a central interlocutor in two of Plato's foundational dialogues, the *Symposium* and the dialogue bearing his name, *Phaedrus*. He represents a vital link in understanding the transmission of Socratic thought and the intellectual currents concerning love, rhetoric, and philosophy in classical Athens during the Peloponnesian War era.

His life took a dramatic turn in 415 BCE, amidst the highly charged political atmosphere of the Peloponnesian War, when he was implicated in the infamous scandal of the profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries. Alongside figures like Alcibiades, Phaedrus was accused of mimicking the sacred rites in private homes, an act considered deeply impious and a threat to the Athenian state. This indictment led to his flight from Athens, likely to Thessaly, where he remained in exile for a period, escaping the severe penalties that befell others involved in the sacrilege, including executions and further exiles.

Through Plato's works, Phaedrus's character has shaped Western understanding of classical Greek thought. In the *Symposium*, he initiates the famous speeches on Eros, articulating a view of love as a divine force inspiring noble deeds. The *Phaedrus* dialogue critically examines the nature of rhetoric, contrasting its manipulative potential with its capacity to serve truth and philosophy, largely through the lens of Phaedrus's own intellectual curiosities and the Socratic method of inquiry. His philosophical legacy, therefore, is primarily an inherited one, refracted through the genius of Plato."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:27:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "443 BCE, Athens"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "443 BCE, Athens"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual ferment in Athens during the 5th century BCE, particularly the rise of sophistry and philosophy, contributed to Phaedrus's engagement with Socrates.", "The political and religious tensions of the Peloponnesian War created a climate where accusations of impiety, such as the profanation of the Eleusinian Mysteries, were used for political ends.", "Plato's decision to feature Phaedrus prominently in his dialogues ensured his enduring historical and philosophical recognition, independent of his direct actions."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-01T15:27:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30705883583", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "392 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "392 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Phaedrus's exile from Ath